@* What a page or a dialog says when the thing it was opened for cannot be shown: a game that
   isn't there, a merge request already answered, a list that failed to load. The three of them
   read as one dead end to a reader — "what I came here for is gone" — so they say it in one
   voice rather than each picking its own.

   The fourth of the app's dead ends, and the only one about a *subject* rather than a list:
   .no-matches is a filtered list the reader emptied, .no-data a panel whose data hasn't happened
   yet, EmptyState a page with nothing on it and the way to fill it. This one is amber because it
   is the only one of the four that is a problem.

   Distinct from Alert, which is a form's voice after it acts and takes the whole FormFeedback;
   this says something the page already knows. *@

<div class="alert alert-warn">
    <Icon Name="alert" Class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 mt-0.5"/>
    <div>@ChildContent</div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What is missing, in a sentence — "Game not found."</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment ChildContent { get; set; } = default!;
}
